lass="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William of Jumièges was a monk of the Norman abbey of Jumièges and the author of the Gesta Normannorum ducum, a history of the dukes of Normandy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-of-modena"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-of-modena.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">William of Modena</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William of Modena was an Italian churchman and papal legate in the Baltic lands from the 1220s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-of-ockham"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-of-ockham.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">William of Ockham</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William of Ockham was an English Franciscan friar and philosopher, one of the leading thinkers of late medieval scholasticism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck"><span class="enc-name">William of Rubruck</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William of Rubruck was a Flemish Franciscan friar who travelled to the Mongol Empire in 1253–1255 on behalf of King Louis IX of France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-of-volpiano"><span class="enc-name">William of Volpiano</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William of Volpiano was an Italian-born Benedictine monk, abbot of Saint-Bénigne in Dijon and a leading figure of the Cluniac reform.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-of-werle"><span class="enc-name">William of Werle</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William of Werle was the last Lord of Werle, a Mecklenburg lordship ruled by a branch of the Nikloting dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-paterson-banker"><span class="enc-name">William Paterson (banker)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William Paterson was a Scottish trader and banker, a founder of the Bank of England in 1694.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-penn"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-penn.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">William Penn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William Penn was an English Quaker and the founder of the Province of Pennsylvania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-phips"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-phips.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">William Phips</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sir William Phips was a Maine-born shipbuilder and treasure hunter who was knighted after recovering silver from a Spanish wreck off Hispaniola in 1687.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-elder"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-pitt-the-elder.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">William Pitt the Elder</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William Pitt the Elder, later 1st Earl of Chatham, was a British statesman who led the government during the Seven Years' War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-pitt-the-younger.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">William Pitt the Younger</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William Pitt the Younger was Prime Minister of Great Britain from 1783 to 1801 and again from 1804 to 1806, taking office at the age of 24.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-prout-maryland"><span class="enc-name">William Prout (Maryland)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William Prout was a settler leader of the colony of Maryland in Africa at Cape Palmas, founded by the Maryland State Colonization Society.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-r-bell"><span class="enc-name">William R. Bell</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William R. Bell was a British colonial officer who served as district officer of Malaita in the British Solomon Islands Protectorate from 1915.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-ruto"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-ruto.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">William Ruto</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William Ruto is a Kenyan politician who has been president of Kenya since 2022.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-sanchez-of-gascony"><span class="enc-name">William Sánchez of Gascony</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">William Sánchez was Duke of Gascony from about 961 until about 997.</span></a></li></ul>
